 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a list of common English adjectives for identification
  const adjectives = /([a-z]+(\s+(jja|jjv|qu)|[mbfnprdglhstwyz]))+/g;

  // Split the sentence into words
  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  let i = 0;
  let j = 1;
  while (i < words.length - 2) {
    // Find the first adjective before the noun
    const match1 = adjectives.exec(words[i]);
    if (match1) {
      const adj1 = match1[0];
      // Find the second adjective before the verb
      const match2 = adjectives.exec(words[j]);
      if (match2 && match2.index > match1.index) {
        // Swap the positions of the two adjectives
        const temp = words[i];
        words[i] = words[j];
        words[j] = temp;
      }
    }
    i++;
    j++;
  }

  // Join the modified word array back into a single string
  return words.join(' ') + '.';
}

// Test the function
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park.")); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

This function identifies adjacent adjectives in a sentence using a regular expression and swaps their positions if they appear in the incorrect order. Note that it may not work perfectly for all sentences, especially those with more complex structures or unusual adjective arrangements. However, it should handle most simple cases like the one you provided.

