 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to smooth the image and then apply the Sobel operator as follows:
```
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter (standard deviation is a parameter that determines the amount of smoothing)
gaussian_filtered_image = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 2);
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```
This modified code first applies a Gaussian filter using `imgaussfilt` function, which takes the grayscale image as input and a standard deviation value as a parameter. The default standard deviation is 1.5, but you can adjust it to suit your needs. Then, the Sobel operator is applied to the smoothed image. This helps reduce noise in the image and improve the accuracy of edge detection.

